TryHackMe: Red Team Engagements
Platform: TryHackMe
Room: Red Team Engagements
Difficulty: Advanced
Date: 2026
Objective
The objective of this room was to understand the structure, planning, and execution phases of Red Team engagements. The focus was on how organizations simulate real-world adversarial attacks to test security controls, improve detection capabilities, and evaluate incident response readiness.
Step 1: Understanding Red Team Operations
I began by studying the purpose of Red Teaming and how it differs from traditional penetration testing.
This covered:
Goal-based adversary simulation
Long-term stealth operations
Testing people, processes, and technology
This demonstrated that Red Team engagements go beyond vulnerability discovery and focus on measuring an organization’s full defensive capability.
Step 2: Rules of Engagement (ROE)
Next, I reviewed the importance of defining Rules of Engagement before any operation begins.
This included:
Scope and target definition
Authorized attack boundaries
Communication procedures
Emergency stop conditions
Legal and compliance considerations
This showed how clear ROE prevents unintended disruption and ensures safe, controlled testing.
Step 3: Reconnaissance and Planning
I then explored the reconnaissance phase used to gather intelligence on the target environment.
This involved:
Open Source Intelligence (OSINT) collection
Identifying attack surfaces
Mapping infrastructure and users
Developing attack paths and objectives
This highlighted how preparation and intelligence gathering are critical for realistic and effective operations.
Step 4: Initial Access Techniques
After planning, I reviewed common methods used to gain initial access.
This included:
Phishing campaigns
Credential attacks
Expliting exposed services
Social engineering opportunities
This demonstrated how attackers often rely on human behavior and misconfigurations rather than only technical exploits.
Step 5: Post-Exploitation & Persistence
Once access is achieved, the focus shifts to maintaining access and achieving objectives.
This covered:
Privilege escalation
Lateral movement
Persistence mechanisms
Data access and exfiltration simulation
Avoiding detection by security teams
This showed how realistic adversary simulation tests blue team monitoring and response capabilities.
Step 6: Reporting and Lessons Learned
Finally, I reviewed how Red Team findings are documented and presented.
This included:
Attack path documentation
Security control effectiveness analysis
Detection and response gaps
Recommendations for remediation and improvement
This emphasized that the goal of Red Teaming is improvement, not simply exploitation.
Conclusion & Outcome
Through this room, I gained a stronger understanding of how Red Team engagements are structured from planning to reporting. This exercise reinforced the importance of controlled adversary simulation in evaluating organizational security maturity.
It also highlighted how security testing must consider not only technical vulnerabilities, but also operational readiness, employee awareness, and incident response effectiveness.
